          "tool_use_id": "toolu_012csGuHwU8nyCV34feMT3nN",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/consistent-hashing.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Consistent Hashing\\ndescription: A distributed hashing scheme that minimizes key remapping when cluster membership changes.\\ntimestamp: 2026-08-17T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Caching\\n  - concept/DistributedSystems\\n  - pattern/LoadBalancing\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source; describes the pattern clearly but without implementation context.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nConsistent hashing maps both cache keys and cache nodes onto the same circular keyspace. When a node is added or removed, only the keys that fall between the changing node and its neighbour are remapped, rather than reshuffling the entire keyspace. This property makes consistent hashing valuable for distributed caching systems where cluster membership changes frequently.\\n\\n## Virtual Nodes\\n\\nA basic consistent hash ring with a small number of physical nodes can lead to uneven load distribution across the cluster. **Virtual nodes** (also called *vnodes*) address this by mapping several ring positions to each physical node, smoothing out the load imbalance that would otherwise result.\\n\\n## Relation to Other Concepts\\n\\n- [[write-ahead-logging]] \\u2014 another technique for durability in distributed systems\\n- Load balancing and cache distribution patterns\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Consistent hashing concepts and practice in distributed cache design.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Good. The log exists and shows a previous entry. The wiki has at least one page already. Now I\u0027ll integrate the new source about consistent hash rings.\n\nThe source describes **consistent hashing** \u2014 a concept from distributed systems that addresses cache scalability. Key points:\n1. Maps cache keys and cache nodes onto a circular keyspace\n2. Adding/removing a node only remaps local keys (not the entire keyspace)\n3. Virtual nodes smooth out load imbalance\n\nThis is clearly a **Concept** page (\u0060concepts/\u0060 folder). Let me create the page.",
